<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Li Yuanhao</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1003–<a href="/years/1048">1048</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> · Also known as Emperor Jingzong of Western Xia</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Li Yuanhao was the founder and first emperor of Western Xia, the Tangut state of north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. In 1028, while still heir, he captured Ganzhou from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uyghurs">Uyghurs</a>, and in 1038 he proclaimed himself emperor of Great Xia. He promoted the Tangut script and won major victories over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> before he was killed by his son in 1048.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Li Yuanhao, also known as Tuoba Yuanhao, Zhao Yuanhao and Weiming Nangxiao, was born on 7 June 1003, the eldest son of the Tangut ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-deming">Li Deming</a>. When his father died in 1032 he became leader of the Tangut. He was described as a talented general who had long wanted to found a state for his people. Early in his leadership he dropped the surname Zhao, which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song dynasty</a> had given the family, and took the surname Weiming. He also changed the Tangut way of life, ordering men to shave their heads on pain of public execution and changing dress and writing.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>With the help of Zhang Yuan and Wu Hao, Chinese who had gone over to him, he took an aggressive stance towards the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a>, and at his height he claimed an army of 500,000 men. In 1034 he attacked the Huanqing territories and captured the Song general Qi Zongju, and from 1036 he campaigned against the Kingdom of Qocho, taking large parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gansu">Gansu</a> from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uyghurs">Uyghurs</a>; the Tangut held the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gansu-corridor">Hexi Corridor</a> for 191 years.</p><p>In 1038 he declared himself emperor, with his capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yinchuan">Xingqing</a>. He then won three large battles against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a>, but at heavy cost, his forces worn down in part by a Song scorched earth policy, and in 1044 a treaty had the Tangut nominally acknowledge Song sovereignty in return for payments from the Song.</p><p>With Han advisers he reorganised the state and created new <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">departments</a>, and he had Chinese works translated by supporting the development of a written Tangut language. He nevertheless opposed imitating the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> too closely, stressing the traditional nomadic way of life and discouraging dependence on Song luxuries.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 1048 the chief minister Mozang Epang and Prince Ningling Ge plotted to kill him. Ningling Ge struck at him with a sword and cut off his nose, then fled to Mozang, who turned him in as the assassin. Jingzong died of his wounds a few days later, on 19 January 1048, and was succeeded by his son Li Liangzuo, Emperor Yizong. The state he founded lasted until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> destroyed it, and its script fell out of use after the Yuan conquest.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Emperor Jingzong of Western Xia” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Western Xia, the Tangut 'Great State of White and High', ruled the Hexi Corridor and the Ordos from <b>Li Yuanhao</b>'s proclamation in 1038, fought the Song to a tributary peace, survived as a fourth power beside Liao and Jin, and built a Buddhist, Confucian-schooled state with its own…</blockquote><a href="/empire/western-xia">Western Xia · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Rising Tangut power closed in, and in 1028 <b>Li Yuanhao</b>, later founder of Western Xia, captured Ganzhou.</blockquote><a href="/state/ganzhou-uyghur-kingdom">Ganzhou Uyghur Kingdom</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Western Xia. Died in Xingqing District, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=38.47,106.28&amp;year=1032" aria-label="Xingqing District on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the place of death of Li Yuanhao"><image href="/maps/globes/g120n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4090 4223h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4090 4223h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=38.47,106.28&amp;year=1032">What was here in 1032? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
